*Tiles.** These are small 3×3 frames with a centre of a different colour, for example a 1-frame with a 4 in the middle. A tile's centre colour always matches the colour of one of the bars.\n\n**What changes.** In each output, every tile is stamped repeatedly with a period of 3, so the copies sit edge to edge. The repetition heads toward the bar whose colour matches the tile's centre, and the copies paint over the bar.\n\n- Pair 1: the tile at (3,3) has centre 4. The 4-bar is to its right, so copies appear at x = 6, 9, 12, 15. The last copy covers columns 15–17, which reaches the bar's far edge (17). The tile at (12,12) has centre 2. Its copies go left at x = 9, 6, 3, and the last one lands inside the 2-bar (columns 1–4).\n- Pair 3: the tile at (8,3) has centre 3. The 3-bar (rows 1–3) is above it and does not share any rows with it, so the copies go **up**, to rows 5 and 2.\n- Pair 2: the tile at (12,13) has centre 4. The 4-bar is below it, so one copy is placed at rows 15–17.\n\n**Choosing the direction.** If the tile's rows overlap the bar's rows, the bar is to the side and the copies run horizontally toward it. Otherwise the copies run vertically toward it.\n\n**When to stop.** Keep stamping one step at a time. Stop as soon as the centre of a copy lies inside the bar. Also never stamp a copy that would extend past the bar's far edge or past the grid edge.\n\nI checked each of these placements by hand against the given outputs (x = 15 then stop; x = 3 then stop; rows 5 and 2 in pair 3; rows 15–17 in pair 2). The reference program implements exactly this logic, and I reuse it.\n\n## Applying it to the test input\n\n| Tile (top-left) | Centre colour | Where the bar is | Copies placed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (7,15) | 4 | right, columns 19–24 | x = 18; centre (8,19) is in the bar, stop |\n| (10,7) | 1 | above, rows 1–2 | y = 7, 4, 1; the copy at y = 1 has its centre in the bar |\n| (13,12) | 2 | above, rows 1–2 | y = 10, 7, 4, 1 |\n| (15,8) | 3 | left, columns 1–3 | x = 5, 2 |\n| (19,12) | 4 | right | x = 15, 18 |\n\nNone of these paths cross each other. Each copy is taken from the original input tile, so the order in which tiles are processed does not matter.\n\n## The program\n\nThere is one emit checkpoint after each tile's run of copies is stamped, and a final emit for the finished grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    # the largest object of each colour is that colour's bar\n    bars = {}\n    for o in objs:\n        c = o[\"colour\"]\n        if c not in bars or o[\"size\"] > bars[c][\"size\"]:\n            bars[c] = o\n    # tiles: 3x3 frames (8 cells) whose centre has another colour that owns a bar\n    tiles = []\n    for o in objs:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        if o[\"size\"] == 8 and y1 - y0 == 2 and x1 - x0 == 2:\n            cc = int(g[y0 + 1, x0 + 1])\n            if cc != o[\"colour\"] and cc != bg and cc in bars:\n                tiles.append((y0, x0, cc))\n    for (y0, x0, cc) in tiles:\n        patch = g[y0:y0 + 3, x0:x0 + 3].copy()\n        by0, bx0, by1, bx1 = bars[cc][\"box\"]\n        if not (y0 + 2 < by0 or y0 > by1):  # rows overlap -> horizontal run\n            if bx0 > x0:\n                dy, dx = 0, 3\n                ok = lambda ny, nx: nx + 2 <= bx1 and nx + 2 < W\n            else:\n                dy, dx = 0, -3\n                ok = lambda ny, nx: nx >= bx0 and nx >= 0\n        else:  # vertical run\n            if by0 > y0:\n                dy, dx = 3, 0\n                ok = lambda ny, nx: ny + 2 <= by1 and ny + 2 < H\n            else:\n                dy, dx = -3, 0\n                ok = lambda ny, nx: ny >= by0 and ny >= 0\n        ny, nx = y0 + dy, x0 + dx\n        while ok(ny, nx):\n            out[ny:ny + 3, nx:nx + 3] = patch\n            if by0 <= ny + 1 <= by1 and bx0 <= nx + 1 <= bx1:\n                break\n            ny += dy\n            nx += dx\n        emit(out, f\"Repeat the tile at ({y0},{x0}) with centre colour {cc} every 3 cells toward the {cc}-bar until a copy's centre lands in the bar.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every tile has been extended into its matching bar.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A tile's centre colour always matches the colour of one of the bars."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What changes.** In each output, every tile is stamped repeatedly with a period of 3, so the copies sit edge to edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The repetition heads toward the bar whose colour matches the tile's centre, and the copies paint over the bar."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What changes.** In each output, every tile is stamped repeatedly with a period of 3, so the copies sit edge to edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**When to stop.** Keep stamping one step at a time."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"There is one emit checkpoint after each tile's run of copies is stamped, and a final emit for the finished grid."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"None of these paths cross each other."}],"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Also never stamp a copy that would extend past the bar's far edge or past the grid edge."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Final grid: every tile has been extended into its matching bar."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Choosing the direction.** If the tile's rows overlap the bar's rows, the bar is to the side and the copies run horizontally toward it."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":true,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"2783b2b3d110ab45febd8155ca9570a54a6470f64c55ac1cb88cc99cbc9f91a0","task_sha256":"35d9e20744781d59cc984492a77eb115d1bc43de1cda28ce9fef9f0baf882d77","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic genera